{:edb/db-before-t 899 :edb/db-after-t 900 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534212 50 #inst "2026-10-06T14:37:13.899Z" 13194139534212 true] [17592186083135 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282629 13194139534212 true] [17592186096539 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282643 13194139534212 true] [17592186096667 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282636 13194139534212 true] [17592186115288 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282650 13194139534212 true] [17592186129238 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282660 13194139534212 true] [17592186147561 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282667 13194139534212 true] [17592186244903 1020 #edb/ref 17592186282657 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282630 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1089 "q-102889" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1100 "How many different codes can be formed by the characters ${y,z,2,4,6}$ besides \"$yz246$\"? Each character can be used only once." 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1113 #uuid "a73f708e-ccdf-5d98-9325-8bdc4f82ae5f" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1142 true 13194139534212 true] [17592186282629 1145 "The total number of the characters is $5$, so there are $5!$ ways of arranging them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe code \"$yz246$\" is one of them. By subtracting $1$ from $120,$ we find the number of the different codes that can be formed beside \"$yz246$\".\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120-1=119\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282631 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282632 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282633 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282634 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282635 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1041 "selection" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282633 13194139534212 true] [17592186282630 1110 #uuid "75328b98-6c82-5e9c-bb1c-60fc6ead4211" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282631 1034 "132" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282631 1111 #uuid "3a766b29-b33e-5bf8-99cc-a9498ac00a4c" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282632 1034 "128" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282632 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282632 1111 #uuid "c5ebdd40-eff2-5962-8c47-618659ba7e61" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282633 1034 "119" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282633 1111 #uuid "3532685c-1e3b-5f20-9f37-c866ae3ee5c7" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282634 1034 "120" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282634 1111 #uuid "ade55d68-ad69-5778-be4b-a2b1ebed97ca" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282635 1034 "124" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282635 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282635 1111 #uuid "62984d86-6720-54f4-83b7-4532c8f1946c" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282636 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282637 13194139534212 true] [17592186282636 1089 "q-150014" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282636 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534212 true] [17592186282636 1100 "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)$ at the point $(0,0).$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282636 1113 #uuid "d44ab2d1-b7c8-5c21-9bb5-01b432266f2a" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282636 1145 "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=3x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\frac{3}{3x+1}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,0)$ is $m=3.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282638 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282639 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282640 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282641 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282642 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1041 "selection" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282638 13194139534212 true] [17592186282637 1110 #uuid "8a438b4a-4bcc-550d-b74d-e7ba2c96cf0d" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282638 1034 "y=3x" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282638 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282638 1111 #uuid "1be6a682-090d-5740-b09a-b04c01b45b58" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282639 1034 "y=3x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282639 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282639 1111 #uuid "32b003f6-061b-5082-b184-04f5019398ad" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282640 1034 "y=3x+1" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282640 1111 #uuid "3b84547a-277e-5d9e-92e4-9b6e76ae57ae" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282641 1034 "y=x" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282641 1111 #uuid "f8ba990d-54a8-5b7b-a159-29a3453dab0a" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282642 1034 "y=x+3" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282642 1111 #uuid "574037b9-99ef-5402-bf32-cbf071bbb2b0" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282643 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282644 13194139534212 true] [17592186282643 1089 "q-1916" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282643 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534212 true] [17592186282643 1100 "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282643 1113 #uuid "77390db0-e13a-5db8-9b13-017e21f5f5f9" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282643 1145 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{x})+({x}^{2})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x+2)x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282645 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282646 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282647 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282648 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282649 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1041 "selection" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282646 13194139534212 true] [17592186282644 1110 #uuid "2fec161b-eca2-5ae3-81da-ac4ebf2dedae" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282645 1034 "x(2{e}^{x}+x)" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282645 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282645 1111 #uuid "352aa2a8-5555-5333-856a-2a46b33d96aa" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282646 1034 "(x+2)x{e}^{x}" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282646 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282646 1111 #uuid "031214be-a46b-5306-9d5f-e82f68bca114" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282647 1034 "x(x{e}^{x}+2)" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282647 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282647 1111 #uuid "3908dc91-dc16-5891-b6f0-f15fdb9d639e" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282648 1034 "(x+1)x{e}^{x}" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282648 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282648 1111 #uuid "e887fe31-888e-5b61-af7c-27507a624531" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282649 1034 "2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282649 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282649 1111 #uuid "cb6dfc71-ac25-5a81-98df-8ff01479f612" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282650 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282651 13194139534212 true] [17592186282650 1089 "q-317288" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282650 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534212 true] [17592186282650 1100 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{5}^{x}+4?$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282650 1113 #uuid "9dea313d-b72c-5519-83b1-358690e83675" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282650 1145 "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/09798cb8c62aabe483490ad24f4c13918138c4292ffb19a8d6612ab4efe18497.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{5}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units up to give $y=-{5}^{x}+4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282652 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282653 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282654 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282655 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282656 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1041 "selection" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282655 13194139534212 true] [17592186282651 1110 #uuid "f165a68f-99a0-52a7-b701-45724d33c861" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282652 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b7c6f2453026a6cdc4dbd91806108513a4522f3aa42bc73e4dcc68e1ae0013f9.png" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282652 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534212 true] [17592186282652 1111 #uuid "63f88284-0fd0-57d6-b2a3-9e8d18e48896" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282653 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/4e70aa30c11b1576699fa384e830d9ad1608963abb0ee98e0cd961d6afb2762c.png" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282653 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534212 true] [17592186282653 1111 #uuid "8e251494-a568-568b-baf1-f74c6dc5609f" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282654 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/72c4b7567924115d3717a14a98d5a1806687b68a6dafe7d102927d98e0d2a4c3.png" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282654 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534212 true] [17592186282654 1111 #uuid "e0f4c91c-03e4-5256-9490-424d241e0d2c" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282655 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/f70c7f91f1164b564fea4582928392748a150e63f66b8038b7aad007e2760034.png" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282655 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534212 true] [17592186282655 1111 #uuid "2001395f-da79-59c1-b806-1e2f7e299f78" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282656 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/78e1b3933cf1048d32fba849cf1fac5e8ef049fc0714cdf0512d912c9875424f.png" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282656 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534212 true] [17592186282656 1111 #uuid "05ec3b62-221d-5583-b938-fa7d81fec805" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282657 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282658 13194139534212 true] [17592186282657 1089 "q-325983" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282657 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534212 true] [17592186282657 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534212 true] [17592186282657 1113 #uuid "ef999424-e263-53f1-bf60-39690b6d18bd" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282657 1145 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{3}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{3}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{3}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282659 13194139534212 true] [17592186282658 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534212 true] [17592186282658 1041 "field-1" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282658 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282659 13194139534212 true] [17592186282658 1110 #uuid "4c5e7501-56e0-5ddf-8a32-892664f3997c" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282659 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282659 1111 #uuid "5101f0dc-70c0-5b62-a2a9-f4da74e5e623" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282660 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282661 13194139534212 true] [17592186282660 1089 "q-40030" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282660 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534212 true] [17592186282660 1100 "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8}{2x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,+\\infty ).$ What is the value of $b?$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282660 1113 #uuid "3b94e205-84ae-5402-93e4-aef9ed193fea" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282660 1145 "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{2}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{2} & =4 \\\\ -b & =8 \\\\ b & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282662 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282663 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282664 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282665 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1000 #edb/ref 17592186282666 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1041 "selection" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1104 #edb/ref 17592186282662 13194139534212 true] [17592186282661 1110 #uuid "e90b041d-9bd3-5018-a809-23259c9b6d38" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282662 1034 "-8" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282662 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282662 1111 #uuid "3a5d195b-bbfd-5035-8e28-dfa550cb4438" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282663 1034 "10" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282663 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282663 1111 #uuid "eae4ba94-e9b8-5bbb-bb69-20ded2bbfb44" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282664 1034 "7" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282664 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282664 1111 #uuid "d94a3d52-0f16-5e25-ab40-30ba4a3ca954" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282665 1034 "5" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282665 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282665 1111 #uuid "91be3907-c716-5987-a5b3-09abc540e46b" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282666 1034 "-6" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282666 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534212 true] [17592186282666 1111 #uuid "0d0f8337-ad59-568e-85d3-ac9595a6c899" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1017 #edb/ref 17592186282668 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1089 "q-62069" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b9668f2d9988488c64e2bb7edb55ae94b1b84b947e249fbc96c506b1c5095d4d.png)\n\nA manufacturer produces a triangular prism-shaped box to hold chocolate candies. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1113 #uuid "7ddd59ad-58ff-5804-a895-8e6dabf22244" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1142 true 13194139534212 true] [17592186282667 1145 "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 24+2\\times 45+54 \\\\  & =48+90+54 \\\\  & =192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534212 true] [17592186282668 1000 #edb